# Netlify redirect / rewrite rules for aseembhandari.com
# Plain text, one rule per line: <from> <to> <status>
# A trailing "!" forces the rule to win even when a real file exists at that path
# (without it, an existing file always beats a rule).
#
# Why this file exists at all: the site deploys with `netlify deploy --dir .`, which
# uploads the whole site folder — including working files that were never meant to be
# public. Nothing is deleted or moved to hide them; they are simply not served.

# --- Working files: uploaded by the deploy, but not public --------------------
# content/ holds article source markdown and the LinkedIn drafts (including posts
# not published yet).
/content/*   /404.html   404!

# The generator scripts, listed one by one on purpose: Netlify only supports "*" as
# a trailing wildcard, so a pattern like /*.py silently matches nothing and serves
# the file (verified on a draft deploy). Add any new root-level script here.
/generate-og-images.py   /404.html   404!
/generate-favicon.py     /404.html   404!

# --- security.txt ------------------------------------------------------------
# RFC 9116 puts the canonical file at /.well-known/security.txt. Serving it as a
# rewrite from the root copy keeps one source file instead of two that can drift —
# and avoids depending on whether the deploy uploads dot-directories at all.
/.well-known/security.txt   /security.txt   200

# --- One URL per page (indexing) ---------------------------------------------
# Netlify serves both /blog-mes-build and /blog-mes-build.html with a 200, so every
# page had two live URLs. The .html form is the one canonical tags, og:url, and
# sitemap.xml all point at, and the one Google already crawled — so the bare form
# 301s to it and there is exactly one indexable URL per page.
#
# Listed one per line because Netlify only supports "*" as a trailing wildcard, and
# a placeholder rule like "/:slug /:slug.html" would also swallow /robots.txt,
# /llms.txt and /favicon.ico. The "!" is needed because Netlify's implicit .html
# resolution counts as an existing file and would otherwise beat these rules.
/index.html        /                      301!
/blog              /blog.html             301!
/blog-mes-build    /blog-mes-build.html   301!
/blog-pilot-build  /blog-pilot-build.html 301!
/blog-yield-signal /blog-yield-signal.html 301!
/blog-spc-system   /blog-spc-system.html  301!
/work-infinitum    /work-infinitum.html   301!
/work-velodyne     /work-velodyne.html    301!
/work-fabrinet     /work-fabrinet.html    301!
/pcbdebug          /pcbdebug.html         301!
/npi-checklist     /npi-checklist.html    301!
/spc-tools         /spc-tools.html        301!
